  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   - Central idea: Develop a hybrid optimization strategy combining deterministic gradient-based methods with probabilistic exploration, incorporating multi-resolution analysis of the shift space to address the highly non-convex landscape. The core innovation is **Shift Importance Sampling (SIS)**, a dimensionality-reduction technique that prioritizes shifts contributing most to the maximum overlap.\n   - Key rationale: The objective function exhibits complex interactions across scales in the shift space. By focusing optimization effort on significant shifts while constraining negligible ones, we can dramatically reduce the effective problem dimension. This contrasts with uniform grid optimization that treats all shifts equally regardless of their contribution.\n   - Bottleneck addressed: Current methods spend excessive time optimizing negligible shifts (<0.1% contribution to C5) trapped in local optima. SIS escapes these regions by strategically ignoring unimportant dimensions.\n   - Distinctions: Unlike simple gradient descent or simulated annealing, SIS uses: (1) rigorous statistical analysis of shift importance, (2) continuous importance weighting, (3) adaptive dimension embedding during optimization, and (4) progressive shift inclusion as the solution evolves to avoid early neglect of critical regions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Representation: Piecewise-constant step function h(x) = {h[0], h[1], ..., h[N-1]} for x \u2208 [0,2]\n   - Variables: h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i, \u2211h[i]\u00b7dx = 1 (dx=2/N)\n   - Objective: min max_k \u222b h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx = (2/N) \u00b7 max_k np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')\n   - Constraints: \n        - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n        - \u2211h[i] = N/2 (after clipping)\n   - Shift importance model: \n        s(k) = (p[k] - m[k]) / (max_k p[k] - min_k m[k])\n        where p[k] = positive contributions, m[k] = negative contributions\n   - Dimension reduction:\n        - Identify top K' shifts by importances s(k)\n        - Transform optimization variables to combine these K' dimensions into lower-dimensional manifold\n        - Parametrize solution as linear combination of carefully chosen basis functions adapted to the shift landscape\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   - Step 1: Parent validation\n        - If parent_h[i] not None:\n            n_orig = len(parent_h)\n            h_init = parent_h.copy().astype(float)\n            dx = 2.0 / n_orig\n        - Else:\n            n_orig = 250  # Fallback resolution\n            h_init = np.ones(n_orig) * 0.5\n        - Project to feasibility: h0 = project_feasible(h_init, n_orig)\n   - Step 2: Importance analysis\n        c_all = compute_c5_vec(h0, n_orig)\n        s_all = np.empty(len(c_all))\n        for k in range(len(c_all)):\n            pos_contrib = np.sum(np.maximum(c_all[k] - np.min(c_all), 0))\n            neg_contrib = np.sum(np.maximum(-np.min(c_all) - c_all[k], 0))\n            shift_range = np.max(c_all) - np.min(c_all)\n            s_all[k] = (pos_contrib + neg_contrib) / shift_range if shift_range > 0 else 0.5\n        sorted_idx = np.argsort(-s_all)\n        top_shifts = sorted_idx[:min(max(50, len(c_all)//4), len(c_all)-1]\n   - Step 3: Dynamic embedding\n        Let M = len(top_shifts)\n        Define low-dimensional embedding: t(x) = sum_{i=1 to p} a_i\u00b7b_{i}(x)\n        where {b_i} form a basis adapted to the shift manifold\n        Optimize over dual variables controlling constraint satisfaction and importance weighting\n        Use block-coordinate ascent: optimize embedded variables while maintaining feasibility projection\n   - Step 4: Progressive enrichment\n        - Monitor improvement trajectory of top k shifted contributions\n        - Gradually include additional shifts (~10% per optimization phase) as embedded manifolds stabilize\n        - Use cross-validation between embedded and full-space solutions to determine enrichment thresholds\n   - Step 5: Projected gradient descent\n        - Custom gradient estimator combining continuous relaxation and importance weighting\n        - Incorporate Nesterov momentum for acceleration\n        - Dynamic learning rates adapting to observed curvature of embedding manifolds\n   - Step 6: Multi-stage optimization\n        500\n        Phase 1 (importance-driven): Optimize embedded 50-point manifold with full importance weighting\n        Phase 2 (enrichment): Incrementally add neglected shifts while pruning saturated ones (~2.5% sample rate)\n        Phase 3 (full-space verification): Maintain separate full-resolution candidate for final validation\n        - Safety: Implicitly included via importance weighting projection at each iteration\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Core configuration:\n        Base manifold dimension p = 12 (determined via cross-entropy analysis)\n        Initial shift inclusion rate = min(0.05 * N, 100)\n        Learning rate decay = 0.9995 (per 100 iterations)\n        Manifold expansion threshold = 10^-6 relative improvement in embedded objective\n   - Variation 1: Basis enrichment - Automatically adjust basis functions as the solution landscape evolves\n   - Variation 2: Multi-timescale annealing - Cooldown 10\u00d7 faster than standard SA for negligible shifts\n   - Variation 3: Topology-informed initialization - Use importance landscape structures to generate 5 candidate initializations\n   - Variation 4: Geometry-aware sampling - Sample candidate shifts with probability weighted by third-derivative information\n        (Trigger when curvature estimators detect non-convex regions)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Total wall-clock (budget_s):\n        - 50% for Phase 1 (importance analysis + initial optimization)\n        - 30% for Phase 2 (progressive enrichment)\n        - 20% for Phase 3 (verification)\n        - Checkpoint durations: Check elapsed time every 50ms relative to remaining budget\n        - Fallback: At any point use least recently pruned shift set (back up to top 5% by importance)\n   - Failure handling:\n        - NaN/inf values: Revert to primal manifold\n        - Out-of-bounds: Apply importance-proportional mass redistribution\n        - Dimension mismatch: Gracefully expand to minimal spherically symmetric basis\n   - Resource estimates:\n        - f(n=500): ~250ms warming up, 50s run time on 10k iterations\n        - f(n=1k): ~50ms, 300s/10k iterations (run 20k at lower p)\n        - Total: ~57s for median case (within 1100s budget)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Feasibility checks (final candidate):\n        1. all(0.0 <= h[i] <= 1.0 for i) must hold\n        2. abs(sum(h) - N/2) <= 1e-6 * max(1, N) (after scaling to target)\n        3. compute_c5(h, N) returns finite value\n   - Secondary checks:\n        - Residual entropy calculation to ensure spread of h[i] (not uniform)\n        - Derivative alignment consistency: check forward/backward gradient estimates\n        - Multi-resolution check: verify solution replicates at key shifts across grids\n   - Return formatting:\n        h_values = np.clip(project_feasible(h_solution, n_points), 0.0, 1.0)\n        c5_bound = compute_c5(h_values, n_points)\n        n_points = len(h_values)\n   - Note: After any intermediate failure, always re-validate using embedded \u2192 full translation projection",
